\[
\int_{k}^{k+1} f(x)\,dx \le f(k) \le \int_{k-1}^{k} f(x)\,dx \qquad (k\ge 2).
\]

Summing these inequalities for \(k=2,\dots,10000\) yields  

\[
\int_{2}^{10001} f(x)\,dx \le \sum_{k=2}^{10000} f(k) \le \int_{1}^{10000} f(x)\,dx .
\]

Hence the upper bound claimed in the theorem,
\[
\sum_{k=2}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{k}} \le \int_{1}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx,
\]
holds true for any antitone function on the given interval. The hypothesis `h_antitone` exactly supplies the required monotonicity, so the statement is mathematically valid and can be proved using the integral test for series.

No logical errors or missing premises are present.
</justification>
