<justification>
For the decreasing function \(f(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) on \([1,10000]\) we have, for each integer \(k\ge 1\),

\[
f(k+1) \;<\; \int_{k}^{k+1} f(x)\,dx \;<\; f(k)
\]

because \(f\) is strictly decreasing (hence antitone) and positive. Summing these inequalities for \(k=1,\dots,9999\) yields  

\[
\sum_{k=2}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{k}}
   \;=\; \sum_{k=1}^{9999} f(k+1)
   \;<\; \int_{1}^{10000} f(x)\,dx .
\]

The integral evaluates to  

\[
\int_{1}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx
   = \Big[2\sqrt{x}\Big]_{1}^{10000}
   = 2(100-1)=198 .
\]

Thus the desired strict inequality holds:

\[
\sum_{k=2}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{k}} \;<\; 198 .
\]

The hypothesis `h_antitone` (that \(f\) is antitone on the interval) is precisely the condition needed to justify the above comparison, so the theorem statement is mathematically correct.
</justification>
