1. [THEOREM] Sum of Indicator-Weighted Terms in a Finite Set: $\sum_{j \in s} \mathbb{1}_{\{i = j\}} f(j) = f(i) \mathbb{1}_{\{i \in s\}}$
Module: Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Ring
Name: Finset.sum_boole_mul
Description: For any finite set $s$ of indices, any function $f : \iota \to \alpha$ (where $\alpha$ is a non-associative semiring), and any index $i \in \iota$, the sum $\sum_{j \in s} \mathbb{1}_{\{i = j\}} \cdot f(j)$ equals $f(i)$ if $i \in s$ and $0$ otherwise. Here $\mathbb{1}_{\{i = j\}}$ is the indicator function that equals $1$ when $i = j$ and $0$ otherwise.
Signature:  (s : Finset ι) (f : ι → α) (i : ι) : ∑ j ∈ s, ite (i = j) 1 0 * f j = ite (i ∈ s) (f i) 0

2. [THEOREM] Evaluation Commutes with Finite Summation of Functions
Module: Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Finsupp
Name: Finset.sum_apply'
Description: For any finite set $s$ and a family of functions $f_k \colon \alpha \to M$ indexed by $k \in s$, the evaluation of the sum of these functions at any point $i \in \alpha$ equals the sum of the evaluations of each function at $i$. That is:
$$ \left(\sum_{k \in s} f_k\right)(i) = \sum_{k \in s} f_k(i) $$
Signature:  : (∑ k ∈ s, f k) i = ∑ k ∈ s, f k i
