4. [INSTANCE] Locally Finite Order Implies Finite Lower-Bounded Intervals
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Finset.Defs
Name: Finset.LocallyFiniteOrder.toLocallyFiniteOrderBot
Description: Every locally finite order is also a locally finite order with finite lower-bounded intervals. That is, if $\alpha$ is a preorder where all bounded intervals are finite, then all intervals of the form $\{x \in \alpha \mid x \leq a\}$ for any $a \in \alpha$ are also finite.
Signature:  : LocallyFiniteOrderBot α

5. [STRUCTURE] Locally finite order
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Finset.Defs
Name: LocallyFiniteOrder
Description: A locally finite order is a preorder $(α, \leq)$ where every bounded interval is finite. That is, for any two elements $a, b \in α$, the set $\{x \in α \mid a \leq x \leq b\}$ is finite. This property allows the intervals $[a,b]$, $[a,b)$, $(a,b]$, and $(a,b)$ to be represented as finite sets (e.g., as finsets in Lean).
Signature:  (α : Type*) [Preorder α]

