\[
\int_{i}^{i+1} f(x)\,dx \;\ge\; f(i+1)
\]

because \(f(x)\le f(i+1)\) for all \(x\in[i,i+1]\). Summing these inequalities for \(i=1,\dots,9999\) yields

\[
\int_{1}^{10000} f(x)\,dx \;\ge\; \sum_{i=1}^{9999} f(i+1)
   = \sum_{i\in\text{Finset.Ico }1\;10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{i+1}} .
\]

Hence the stated inequality

\[
\sum_{i\in\text{Finset.Ico }1\;10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{i+1}}
   \le \int_{1}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx
\]

holds. The provided hypotheses (`h_antitone`, `h_pointwise`, `h_integrable`) are indeed true for this function, so the theorem is provable from them. No logical errors or missing assumptions are present.
