<justification>
The function \(f(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) is strictly decreasing on \([1,10000]\); hence it is antitone on that interval, which is exactly the given hypothesis `h_antitone`.  

For a strictly decreasing positive function we have the standard integral test inequalities  

\[
\int_{2}^{10001} f(x)\,dx \;<\; \sum_{k=2}^{10000} f(k) \;<\; \int_{1}^{10000} f(x)\,dx .
\]

Evaluating the integrals gives  

\[
\int_{1}^{10000} \frac{dx}{\sqrt{x}} = 2(\sqrt{10000}-\sqrt{1}) = 198,
\]
\[
\int_{2}^{10001} \frac{dx}{\sqrt{x}} = 2(\sqrt{10001}-\sqrt{2}) \approx 197.18.
\]

Thus  

\[
197.18\; <\; \sum_{k=2}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{k}} \;<\; 198 .
\]

The sum is strictly less than the integral, so they cannot be equal. Consequently the statement  

\[
\bigl(\sum_{k=2}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{k}}\bigr) \neq \int_{1}^{10000} \frac{dx}{\sqrt{x}}
\]

is mathematically correct and follows from the antitone hypothesis. No logical errors are present.
</justification>
