The function \(f(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) is positive and decreasing on \([1,10000]\) (the hypothesis `h_antitone`).  
For each integer \(i\) with \(1\le i<10000\) and each \(x\in[i,i+1)\) we have  
\[
\frac{1}{\sqrt{i+1}}\le \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}},
\]
which is exactly the content of `h_pointwise`.  

Consequently, on each sub‑interval \([i,i+1]\) the integral of \(f\) satisfies  

\[
\int_{i}^{i+1} f(x)\,dx \;\ge\; f(i+1)\cdot (i+1-i)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{i+1}} .
\]

Summing these inequalities over all \(i\in\{1,\dots,9999\}\) (i.e. over `Finset.Ico (1) 10000`) yields  

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{9999} \frac{1}{\sqrt{i+1}}
\;\le\;
\sum_{i=1}^{9999}\int_{i}^{i+1} f(x)\,dx
=
\int_{1}^{10000} f(x)\,dx .
\]

The additional hypotheses (`h_integrable`, `h_int_Ico`, `hab`) guarantee that the integral exists, but they do not affect the inequality itself. Hence the claimed inequality is a correct mathematical statement and can be proved from the given premises.
</justification>
