1. [THEOREM] Integrable Functions are Interval Integrable
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral
Name: MeasureTheory.Integrable.intervalIntegrable
Description: If a function $f : \mathbb{R} \to E$ is integrable with respect to a measure $\mu$ on $\mathbb{R}$, then $f$ is interval integrable with respect to $\mu$ on any interval $[a, b]$, i.e., $f$ is integrable on both $(a, b]$ and $(b, a]$.
Signature:  (hf : Integrable f μ) : IntervalIntegrable f μ a b

2. [THEOREM] Nonzero Interval Integral Implies Interval Integrability
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral
Name: intervalIntegral.intervalIntegrable_of_integral_ne_zero
Description: For any real numbers $a$ and $b$, and any function $f \colon \mathbb{R} \to E$, if the interval integral $\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \, d\mu$ is nonzero, then $f$ is interval integrable with respect to the measure $\mu$ on the interval from $a$ to $b$.
Signature:  {a b : ℝ} {f : ℝ → E} {μ : Measure ℝ} (h : (∫ x in a..b, f x ∂μ) ≠ 0) : IntervalIntegrable f μ a b

3. [DEFINITION] Interval integrability of a function
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral
Name: IntervalIntegrable
Description: A function $f : \mathbb{R} \to E$ is called *interval integrable* with respect to a measure $\mu$ on an interval from $a$ to $b$ if it is integrable on both intervals $(a, b]$ and $(b, a]$. Since one of these intervals is always empty, this is equivalent to $f$ being integrable on $(\min(a, b), \max(a, b)]$.
Signature:  (f : ℝ → E) (μ : Measure ℝ) (a b : ℝ) : Prop

4. [THEOREM] Integrability on Closed Interval Implies Interval Integrability
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral
Name: MeasureTheory.IntegrableOn.intervalIntegrable
Description: If a function $f : \mathbb{R} \to E$ is integrable on the closed interval $[a, b]$ with respect to a measure $\mu$, then $f$ is interval integrable on $[a, b]$ with respect to $\mu$, i.e., $f$ is integrable on both $(a, b]$ and $(b, a]$.
Signature:  (hf : IntegrableOn f [[a, b]] μ) : IntervalIntegrable f μ a b

