2. [THEOREM] Scaling and Reflection Property of Interval Integral: $\int_{a}^{b} f(d - c x) \, dx = c^{-1} \int_{d - c b}^{d - c a} f(x) \, dx$
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral
Name: intervalIntegral.integral_comp_sub_mul
Description: For any nonzero real number $c \neq 0$ and any real number $d$, the integral of the function $f(d - c \cdot x)$ over the interval $[a, b]$ is equal to $c^{-1}$ times the integral of $f(x)$ over the interval $[d - c \cdot b, d - c \cdot a]$. That is,
\[ \int_{a}^{b} f(d - c \cdot x) \, dx = c^{-1} \cdot \int_{d - c \cdot b}^{d - c \cdot a} f(x) \, dx. \]
Signature:  (hc : c ≠ 0) (d) : (∫ x in a..b, f (d - c * x)) = c⁻¹ • ∫ x in d - c * b..d - c * a, f x

