Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral
Name: intervalIntegral.integral_comp_div_add
Description: For any nonzero real number $c \neq 0$ and any real number $d$, the integral of the function $f\left(\frac{x}{c} + d\right)$ over the interval $[a, b]$ equals $c$ times the integral of $f(x)$ over the interval $\left[\frac{a}{c} + d, \frac{b}{c} + d\right]$. That is,
\[ \int_{a}^{b} f\left(\frac{x}{c} + d\right) \, dx = c \int_{\frac{a}{c} + d}^{\frac{b}{c} + d} f(x) \, dx. \]
Signature:  (hc : c ≠ 0) (d) : (∫ x in a..b, f (x / c + d)) = c • ∫ x in a / c + d..b / c + d, f x

4. [THEOREM] Sum of Indicator-Weighted Terms in a Finite Set: $\sum_{j \in s} \mathbb{1}_{\{i = j\}} f(j) = f(i) \mathbb{1}_{\{i \in s\}}$
Module: Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Ring
Name: Finset.sum_boole_mul
Description: For any finite set $s$ of indices, any function $f : \iota \to \alpha$ (where $\alpha$ is a non-associative semiring), and any index $i \in \iota$, the sum $\sum_{j \in s} \mathbb{1}_{\{i = j\}} \cdot f(j)$ equals $f(i)$ if $i \in s$ and $0$ otherwise. Here $\mathbb{1}_{\{i = j\}}$ is the indicator function that equals $1$ when $i = j$ and $0$ otherwise.
Signature:  (s : Finset ι) (f : ι → α) (i : ι) : ∑ j ∈ s, ite (i = j) 1 0 * f j = ite (i ∈ s) (f i) 0

