1. [THEOREM] Characterization of Antitone Functions on Finite Sets via Insertion
Module: Mathlib.Data.Finset.Interval
Name: Finset.antitone_iff_forall_insert_le
Description: A function $f$ from the finite sets of a type $\alpha$ to a preorder is antitone if and only if for every finite set $s \subseteq \alpha$ and every element $a \notin s$, we have $f(s \cup \{a\}) \leq f(s)$.
Signature:  : Antitone f ↔ ∀ s ⦃a⦄, a ∉ s → f (insert a s) ≤ f s

2. [THEOREM] Constant Functions are Antitone on Any Subset
Module: Mathlib.Order.Monotone.Basic
Name: antitoneOn_const
Description: For any constant function $f \colon \alpha \to \beta$ between two preorders, where $f(x) = c$ for some fixed $c \in \beta$, and for any subset $s \subseteq \alpha$, the function $f$ is antitone on $s$. That is, for all $a, b \in s$, if $a \leq b$, then $c \leq c$.
Signature:  [Preorder α] [Preorder β] {c : β} {s : Set α} : AntitoneOn (fun _ : α ↦ c) s

3. [THEOREM] Strictly Antitone Functions are Antitone
Module: Mathlib.Order.Monotone.Basic
Name: StrictAnti.antitone
Description: If a function $f \colon \alpha \to \beta$ between two preorders is strictly antitone (i.e., $a < b$ implies $f(b) < f(a)$), then $f$ is also antitone (i.e., $a \leq b$ implies $f(b) \leq f(a)$).
Signature:  (hf : StrictAnti f) : Antitone f

