1. [THEOREM] Integral of Antitone Function on Interval Bounded by Sum Over Integer Points
Module: Mathlib.Analysis.SumIntegralComparisons
Name: AntitoneOn.integral_le_sum_Ico
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be an antitone (decreasing) function on the closed interval $[a, b]$, where $a \leq b$. Then the integral of $f$ over $[a, b]$ is bounded above by the sum of $f$ evaluated at the integer points in the half-open interval $[a, b)$, i.e.,
\[
\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \, dx \leq \sum_{x \in [a, b) \cap \mathbb{Z}} f(x).
\]
Signature:  (hab : a ≤ b) (hf : AntitoneOn f (Set.Icc a b)) : (∫ x in a..b, f x) ≤ ∑ x ∈ Finset.Ico a b, f x

2. [THEOREM] Sum of Antitone Function at Right Endpoints is Bounded by Integral over Interval
Module: Mathlib.Analysis.SumIntegralComparisons
Name: AntitoneOn.sum_le_integral_Ico
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be an antitone (decreasing) function on the closed interval $[a, b]$, where $a \leq b$. Then the sum of the values of $f$ at the right endpoints of the integer subintervals in $[a, b)$, i.e., $f(i+1)$ for each integer $i$ in the half-open interval $[a, b)$, is less than or equal to the integral of $f$ over $[a, b]$. Mathematically, this is expressed as:
\[
\sum_{i \in [a, b) \cap \mathbb{Z}} f(i+1) \leq \int_{a}^{b} f(x) \, dx.
\]
Signature:  (hab : a ≤ b) (hf : AntitoneOn f (Set.Icc a b)) : (∑ i ∈ Finset.Ico a b, f (i + 1 : ℕ)) ≤ ∫ x in a..b, f x

1. [DEFINITION] Congruence application in `conv` mode
Module: Mathlib.Tactic.ApplyCongr
Name: Lean.Elab.Tactic.applyCongr
Description: The tactic `apply_congr` applies congruence lemmas within `conv` mode, particularly useful when automatically generated congruence lemmas are not optimal. It can be called with or without an argument: without an argument, it tries all lemmas marked with `@[congr]`; with an argument `e`, it applies the lemma `e`. 

In `conv` mode, a goal `∣ X` represents `⊢ X = ?m` (an equation with a metavariable for the right-hand side). The lemma `e` must unify with such a goal, and its right-hand side determines the metavariable. New goals introduced by `apply_congr` must be equations with a metavariable on the right-hand side; otherwise, the tactic fails. 

A key use case is rewriting inside the operand of a `Finset.sum`, where `apply_congr` provides an additional hypothesis (e.g., `x ∈ S`) to facilitate the rewrite.
Signature:  (q : Option Expr) : TacticM Unit

2. [THEOREM] Evaluation of Finite Sum of Dependently-Typed Functions is Pointwise Sum of Evaluations
Module: Mathlib.Data.DFinsupp.BigOperators
Name: DFinsupp.finset_sum_apply
Description: For any family of additive commutative monoids $(\beta_i)_{i \in \iota}$, any finite set $s$ of elements of type $\alpha$, and any function $g \colon \alpha \to \Pi_{i \in \iota} \beta_i$ mapping each element of $\alpha$ to a dependently-typed function with finite support, the evaluation at index $i$ of the sum $\sum_{a \in s} g(a)$ equals the sum $\sum_{a \in s} g(a)(i)$ for each $i \in \iota$.
Signature:  {α} [∀ i, AddCommMonoid (β i)] (s : Finset α) (g : α → Π₀ i, β i) (i : ι) : (∑ a ∈ s, g a) i = ∑ a ∈ s, g a i

3. [THEOREM] Sum of Indicator-Weighted Terms in a Finite Set: $\sum_{j \in s} \mathbb{1}_{\{i = j\}} f(j) = f(i) \mathbb{1}_{\{i \in s\}}$
Module: Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Ring
Name: Finset.sum_boole_mul
Description: For any finite set $s$ of indices, any function $f : \iota \to \alpha$ (where $\alpha$ is a non-associative semiring), and any index $i \in \iota$, the sum $\sum_{j \in s} \mathbb{1}_{\{i = j\}} \cdot f(j)$ equals $f(i)$ if $i \in s$ and $0$ otherwise. Here $\mathbb{1}_{\{i = j\}}$ is the indicator function that equals $1$ when $i = j$ and $0$ otherwise.
Signature:  (s : Finset ι) (f : ι → α) (i : ι) : ∑ j ∈ s, ite (i = j) 1 0 * f j = ite (i ∈ s) (f i) 0

4. [THEOREM] Evaluation Commutes with Finite Summation of Functions
Module: Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Finsupp
Name: Finset.sum_apply'
Description: For any finite set $s$ and a family of functions $f_k \colon \alpha \to M$ indexed by $k \in s$, the evaluation of the sum of these functions at any point $i \in \alpha$ equals the sum of the evaluations of each function at $i$. That is:
$$ \left(\sum_{k \in s} f_k\right)(i) = \sum_{k \in s} f_k(i) $$
Signature:  : (∑ k ∈ s, f k) i = ∑ k ∈ s, f k i

