[2026-09-05 04:56:26,970][src.models.HILBERTWorker][INFO] - The subgoal is mathematically correct! Full response:
[2026-09-05 04:56:26,970][src.models.HILBERTWorker][INFO] - YES
<justification>
The function \(f(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) is positive and strictly decreasing on the interval \([1,10000]\). For a decreasing function we have the standard estimate  

\[
\forall k\in\mathbb{N},\; \int_{k}^{k+1} f(x)\,dx \ge f(k+1),
\]

because on \([k,k+1]\) the function values are bounded below by the right‑endpoint value \(f(k+1)\). Summing this inequality over \(k=1,\dots,9999\) yields  

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{9999} \frac{1}{\sqrt{i+1}}
   \le \int_{1}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx .
\]

The hypotheses supplied in the theorem (antitone on the interval, pointwise inequality, integrability, etc.) precisely formalise the conditions needed for this comparison argument. Hence the conclusion follows and the statement is mathematically correct.
</justification>
