[2026-09-05 05:18:24,328][src.models.HILBERTWorker][INFO] - YES
<justification>
For the decreasing positive function \(f(x)=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) on the interval \([1,10000]\) we have the standard estimate for Riemann sums of a monotone function:

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} f(i+1)\;=\;\sum_{i\in\text{Ico}(1,n)} f(i+1)
\;\le\;
\int_{1}^{n} f(x)\,dx,
\]

because each term \(f(i+1)\) is the value of \(f\) at the right endpoint of the sub‑interval \([i,i+1]\) and, for a decreasing function, the right‑endpoint rectangle underestimates the area under the curve.

The hypothesis `h_antitone` asserts exactly that \(f\) is antitone (i.e. decreasing) on the closed interval \([1,10000]\). Hence the inequality

\[
\sum_{i\in\text{Finset.Ico }1\;10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{i+1}}
\;\le\;
\int_{1}^{10000} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx
\]

holds true. No contradictory assumptions are present, and the statement follows from a standard monotonicity argument, so the theorem is mathematically correct.
</justification>
