The function \(f(x)=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) is positive and decreasing on \([1,\infty)\), so the given premise that it is antitone on \([1,10000]\) holds (though it is not needed for the conclusion).

The integral can be evaluated explicitly:
\[
\int_{1}^{2}\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx
= \Bigl[2\sqrt{x}\Bigr]_{1}^{2}
= 2\sqrt{2}-2.
\]

Numerically,
\[
2\sqrt{2}-2 \approx 2\cdot1.4142-2 = 0.8284\ldots,
\qquad
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \approx 0.7071\ldots
\]

Since \(2\sqrt{2}-2 \neq \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\), the statement
\[
\int_{1}^{2}\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx \neq \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\]
is correct. Therefore the theorem is mathematically valid and can be proved (by evaluating the integral and comparing the two real numbers).

No correction is needed.
</justification>
