1. [THEOREM] Subset Criterion for Unordered Intervals via Membership
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Finset.Basic
Name: Finset.uIcc_subset_uIcc_iff_mem
Description: For any elements $a₁, a₂, b₁, b₂$ in a lattice $\alpha$, the unordered interval $\text{uIcc}(a₁, b₁)$ is a subset of the unordered interval $\text{uIcc}(a₂, b₂)$ if and only if both $a₁$ and $b₁$ belong to $\text{uIcc}(a₂, b₂)$. Here, $\text{uIcc}(a, b)$ denotes the set $\{x \in \alpha \mid a \sqcap b \leq x \leq a \sqcup b\}$.
Signature:  : [[a₁, b₁]] ⊆ [[a₂, b₂]] ↔ a₁ ∈ [[a₂, b₂]] ∧ b₁ ∈ [[a₂, b₂]]

2. [THEOREM] Lebesgue Measure of Interval: $\text{volume}(\text{uIcc}(a, b)) = |b - a|$
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Measure.Lebesgue.Basic
Name: Real.volume_interval
Description: For any real numbers $a$ and $b$, the Lebesgue measure of the interval $\text{uIcc}(a, b)$ (the closed interval between $a$ and $b$) is equal to the absolute value of their difference, $|b - a|$.
Signature:  {a b : ℝ} : volume (uIcc a b) = ofReal |b - a|

3. [THEOREM] Lebesgue Measure of Open Interval: $\text{volume}((a, b)) = b - a$
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Measure.Lebesgue.Basic
Name: Real.volume_Ioo
Description: For any real numbers $a < b$, the Lebesgue measure of the open interval $(a, b)$ is equal to $b - a$, i.e., $\text{volume}((a, b)) = b - a$.
Signature:  {a b : ℝ} : volume (Ioo a b) = ofReal (b - a)

4. [THEOREM] Membership in Unordered Interval via Ordering: $a \leq x \leq b \implies x \in \text{uIcc}(a, b)$
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Finset.Basic
Name: Finset.mem_uIcc_of_le
Description: For any elements $a, b, x$ in a lattice $\alpha$, if $a \leq x$ and $x \leq b$, then $x$ belongs to the unordered closed interval $\text{uIcc}(a, b) = \{y \in \alpha \mid a \sqcap b \leq y \leq a \sqcup b\}$.
Signature:  (ha : a ≤ x) (hb : x ≤ b) : x ∈ [[a, b]]

5. [THEOREM] Inclusion of Unordered Intervals under Membership Conditions
Module: Mathlib.Order.Interval.Finset.Basic
Name: Finset.uIcc_subset_uIcc
Description: For any elements $a_1, a_2, b_1, b_2$ in a lattice $\alpha$, if $a_1$ and $b_1$ belong to the unordered interval $\text{uIcc}(a_2, b_2)$, then the unordered interval $\text{uIcc}(a_1, b_1)$ is a subset of $\text{uIcc}(a_2, b_2)$. Here, $\text{uIcc}(a, b)$ denotes the set $\{x \in \alpha \mid a \sqcap b \leq x \leq a \sqcup b\}$.
Signature:  (h₁ : a₁ ∈ [[a₂, b₂]]) (h₂ : b₁ ∈ [[a₂, b₂]]) : [[a₁, b₁]] ⊆ [[a₂, b₂]]

