<justification>
The function \(f(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) is decreasing on \([1,2]\), so the premise `AntitoneOn (fun x => 1 / Real.sqrt x) (Set.Icc 1 2)` holds. Moreover, \(f\) is not constant on that interval (e.g., \(f(1)=1\neq f(2)=1/\sqrt{2}\)), so the second premise is also satisfied.

The integral can be computed explicitly:
\[
\int_{1}^{2} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx
= \int_{1}^{2} x^{-1/2}\,dx
= \bigl[2\sqrt{x}\bigr]_{1}^{2}
= 2(\sqrt{2}-1)=2\sqrt{2}-2.
\]
Numerically, \(2\sqrt{2}-2 \approx 0.8284\).

The right‑hand side is \(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\approx 0.7071\).

Since \(0.8284 > 0.7071\), we have
\[
\int_{1}^{2} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx > \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}.
\]

Thus the conclusion follows from the given premises, and the theorem statement is mathematically correct.
</justification>
