1. [THEOREM] Monotonicity of Set Integral under Almost Everywhere Inequality (Restricted Measure)
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.SetIntegral
Name: MeasureTheory.setIntegral_mono_ae_restrict
Description: Let $f$ and $g$ be measurable functions on a measure space $(X, \mu)$, and let $s$ be a measurable subset of $X$. If $f \leq g$ holds almost everywhere on $s$ with respect to the restricted measure $\mu \restrict s$, then the integral of $f$ over $s$ is less than or equal to the integral of $g$ over $s$, i.e.,
\[ \int_{x \in s} f(x) \, d\mu \leq \int_{x \in s} g(x) \, d\mu. \]
Signature:  (h : f ≤ᵐ[μ.restrict s] g) : ∫ x in s, f x ∂μ ≤ ∫ x in s, g x ∂μ

2. [THEOREM] Strict Monotonicity of Restricted Lower Lebesgue Integral on Non-Null Measurable Set
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.Lebesgue
Name: MeasureTheory.setLIntegral_strict_mono
Description: Let $f, g \colon \alpha \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \cup \{\infty\}$ be functions and $\mu$ a measure on $\alpha$ such that:
1. $s \subseteq \alpha$ is a measurable set with $\mu(s) \neq 0$,
2. $g$ is measurable,
3. The restricted integral $\int_{-s} f \, d\mu$ is finite, and
4. For almost all $x \in \alpha$, if $x \in s$ then $f(x) < g(x)$.

Then the strict inequality $\int_{-s} f \, d\mu < \int_{-s} g \, d\mu$ holds.
Signature:  {f g : α → ℝ≥0∞} {s : Set α} (hsm : MeasurableSet s) (hs : μ s ≠ 0) (hg : Measurable g) (hfi : ∫⁻ x in s, f x ∂μ ≠ ∞)
  (h : ∀ᵐ x ∂μ, x ∈ s → f x < g x) : ∫⁻ x in s, f x ∂μ < ∫⁻ x in s, g x ∂μ
