<justification>
The hypothesis states that almost everywhere on the interval \([1,2]\) we have  

\[
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\le \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}} .
\]

For every \(x\in[1,2]\) we indeed have \(\sqrt{x}\le\sqrt{2}\), hence \(\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\ge\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\). Thus the inequality holds pointwise (and therefore a.e.).  

Both functions are integrable on the finite interval \([1,2]\). By the monotonicity of the Lebesgue integral, if \(f\ge g\) a.e. on a measurable set, then  

\[
\int_{1}^{2} f \ge \int_{1}^{2} g .
\]

Taking \(f(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) and \(g(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\) yields exactly the desired conclusion:

\[
\int_{1}^{2} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx \;\ge\; \int_{1}^{2} \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,dx .
\]

The slight difference between the sets used in the hypothesis (\(\mathrm{Icc}\)) and in the integral notation (\(\mathrm{Ioc}\)) is irrelevant, because the two sets differ only by the single point \(x=1\), which has Lebesgue measure zero. Hence the hypothesis also implies the inequality a.e. on the domain of integration.

Consequently the theorem statement is mathematically correct and provable from the given premise.
</justification>
