<justification>
The integrand is \(f(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) on the interval \([1,2]\).  
Its integral is  

\[
\int_{1}^{2}\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx
= \Bigl[2\sqrt{x}\Bigr]_{1}^{2}
=2(\sqrt{2}-1)\approx0.8284.
\]

The constant on the right‑hand side is \(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\approx0.7071\).  
Hence  

\[
\int_{1}^{2}\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx > \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} .
\]

The hypothesis  

\[
h_{\text{strict\_ae}}:\;
\forall^{\!ae}x\in[1,2],\;
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} < \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}
\]

holds because for every \(x\in[1,2)\) the inequality is strict, and at the single point \(x=2\) we have equality; thus it is true almost everywhere.  
From the a.e. inequality we obtain \(f(x)\ge \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\) a.e. and \(f(x)>\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\) on a set of positive measure, which yields  

\[
\int_{1}^{2} f(x)\,dx \;>\; \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\cdot\operatorname{vol}([1,2]) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} .
\]

Therefore the conclusion of the theorem follows, and the statement is mathematically correct.
</justification>
