1. [THEOREM] Monotonicity of Lower Lebesgue Integral for Nonnegative Real-Valued Functions
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.Lebesgue
Name: MeasureTheory.lintegral_mono_nnreal
Description: For any measurable functions $f, g \colon \alpha \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ with $f \leq g$ pointwise, the lower Lebesgue integrals satisfy:
\[
\int⁻_a f(a) \, d\mu \leq \int⁻_a g(a) \, d\mu.
\]
Signature:  {f g : α → ℝ≥0} (h : f ≤ g) : ∫⁻ a, f a ∂μ ≤ ∫⁻ a, g a ∂μ

2. [THEOREM] Monotonicity of Lower Lebesgue Integral Almost Everywhere
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.Lebesgue
Name: MeasureTheory.lintegral_mono_ae
Description: For any two measurable functions $f, g \colon \alpha \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \cup \{\infty\}$ and a measure $\mu$ on $\alpha$, if $f(a) \leq g(a)$ holds $\mu$-almost everywhere, then the lower Lebesgue integral of $f$ with respect to $\mu$ is less than or equal to that of $g$, i.e.,
\[ \int⁻_a f(a) \, d\mu \leq \int⁻_a g(a) \, d\mu. \]
Signature:  {f g : α → ℝ≥0∞} (h : ∀ᵐ a ∂μ, f a ≤ g a) : ∫⁻ a, f a ∂μ ≤ ∫⁻ a, g a ∂μ

3. [THEOREM] Integrability of Constant Function: $f \equiv c$ is integrable iff $c = 0$ or $\mu$ is finite
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Function.L1Space.Integrable
Name: MeasureTheory.integrable_const_iff
Description: For a constant function $f(\omega) = c$ where $c$ is an element of a normed additive commutative group $\beta$, the following are equivalent:
1. $f$ is integrable with respect to a measure $\mu$ on $\alpha$
2. Either $c = 0$ or $\mu$ is a finite measure (i.e., $\mu(\alpha) < \infty$)

In mathematical notation:
\[ \text{Integrable}(f, \mu) \iff (c = 0 \lor \text{IsFiniteMeasure}(\mu)) \]
Signature:  {c : β} : Integrable (fun _ : α => c) μ ↔ c = 0 ∨ IsFiniteMeasure μ

4. [THEOREM] Integrability of Constant Functions: $C = 0$ or Finite Measure
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntegrableOn
Name: MeasureTheory.integrableOn_const
Description: For a constant function $f(x) = C$ where $C$ is an element of a normed space $E$, and a measure $\mu$ on a measurable space, the function $f$ is integrable on a set $s$ with respect to $\mu$ if and only if either $C = 0$ or the measure $\mu$ is finite on $s$ (i.e., $\mu(s) < \infty$).
Signature:  {C : E} : IntegrableOn (fun _ => C) s μ ↔ C = 0 ∨ μ s < ∞

5. [THEOREM] Monotonicity of Lower Lebesgue Integral with Respect to Pointwise Inequality
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.Lebesgue
Name: MeasureTheory.lintegral_mono_fn
Description: For any measurable functions $f, g \colon \alpha \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \cup \{\infty\}$ such that $f(x) \leq g(x)$ for all $x \in \alpha$, the lower Lebesgue integrals satisfy:
\[
\int⁻_a f(a) \, d\mu \leq \int⁻_a g(a) \, d\mu.
\]
Signature:  ⦃f g : α → ℝ≥0∞⦄ (hfg : ∀ x, f x ≤ g x) : ∫⁻ a, f a ∂μ ≤ ∫⁻ a, g a ∂μ

1. [THEOREM] Monotonicity of interval integral under almost everywhere inequality
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral
Name: intervalIntegral.integral_mono_ae
