Description: Let $\mu$ be a finite measure on a measurable space $\alpha$ and let $s \subseteq \alpha$ be a null-measurable set. Then the property that almost every element $a$ (with respect to $\mu$) belongs to $s$ is equivalent to the measure of $s$ being equal to the measure of the entire space $\alpha$, i.e.,
\[
(\forall a \text{ a.e. } \mu, a \in s) \leftrightarrow \mu(s) = \mu(\text{univ}).
\]
Signature:  [IsFiniteMeasure μ] {s : Set α} (hs : NullMeasurableSet s μ) : (∀ᵐ a ∂μ, a ∈ s) ↔ μ s = μ univ

2. [THEOREM] Characterization of Almost Everywhere Truth via Null Set Complement
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.OuterMeasure.AE
Name: MeasureTheory.ae_iff
Description: For any predicate $p$ on a type $\alpha$ and any measure $\mu$ on $\alpha$, the statement $\forall^a x \partial \mu, p(x)$ (read as "$p$ holds $\mu$-almost everywhere") is equivalent to the condition that the $\mu$-measure of the set $\{x \in \alpha \mid \neg p(x)\}$ is zero.
Signature:  {p : α → Prop} : (∀ᵐ a ∂μ, p a) ↔ μ {a | ¬p a} = 0

3. [THEOREM] Almost Everywhere Property on Union of Sets Equals Conjunction of Component Properties
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Measure.Restrict
Name: MeasureTheory.ae_restrict_union_iff
Description: For any subsets $s$ and $t$ of a measurable space $\alpha$ and any property $p : \alpha \to \text{Prop}$, the property $p$ holds $\mu$-almost everywhere on the restriction of $\mu$ to $s \cup t$ if and only if $p$ holds $\mu$-almost everywhere on both $\mu|_s$ and $\mu|_t$. 

In other words:
\[ \left(\forall^\mu x \in s \cup t, p(x)\right) \leftrightarrow \left(\forall^\mu x \in s, p(x)\right) \land \left(\forall^\mu x \in t, p(x)\right) \]
Signature:  (s t : Set α) (p : α → Prop) : (∀ᵐ x ∂μ.restrict (s ∪ t), p x) ↔ (∀ᵐ x ∂μ.restrict s, p x) ∧ ∀ᵐ x ∂μ.restrict t, p x

4. [THEOREM] Characterization of Almost Everywhere Set Inequality via Measure Zero Difference
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.OuterMeasure.AE
Name: MeasureTheory.ae_le_set
Description: For any two sets $s$ and $t$ in a measure space with measure $\mu$, the relation $s \leq_{\text{a.e.} t$ holds (i.e., $s$ is almost everywhere less than or equal to $t$) if and only if the measure of the set difference $s \setminus t$ is zero, i.e., $\mu(s \setminus t) = 0$.
Signature:  : s ≤ᵐ[μ] t ↔ μ (s \ t) = 0

