[2026-09-05 06:39:18,346][src.models.HILBERTWorker][INFO] - 
1. [THEOREM] Interval Integrability of Reciprocal of Nonzero Continuous Functions
Module: Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Integrals
Name: intervalIntegral.intervalIntegrable_one_div
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function on the closed interval $[\min(a, b), \max(a, b)]$ such that $f(x) \neq 0$ for all $x \in [a, b]$. Then the function $x \mapsto 1 / f(x)$ is interval integrable on $[a, b]$ with respect to any measure $\mu$.
Signature:  (h : ∀ x : ℝ, x ∈ [[a, b]] → f x ≠ 0) (hf : ContinuousOn f [[a, b]]) : IntervalIntegrable (fun x => 1 / f x) μ a b

2. [THEOREM] Set Integral is Bounded by Integral Over Nonnegative Part
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.SetIntegral
Name: MeasureTheory.setIntegral_le_nonneg
Description: For any measurable set $s$ and strongly measurable function $f$ that is integrable with respect to measure $\mu$, the integral of $f$ over $s$ is less than or equal to the integral of $f$ over the set $\{y \mid 0 \leq f(y)\}$. That is,
\[ \int_{x \in s} f(x) \, d\mu \leq \int_{x \in \{y \mid 0 \leq f(y)\}} f(x) \, d\mu. \]
Signature:  {s : Set X} (hs : MeasurableSet s) (hf : StronglyMeasurable f) (hfi : Integrable f μ) :
  ∫ x in s, f x ∂μ ≤ ∫ x in {y | 0 ≤ f y}, f x ∂μ

3. [THEOREM] Interval integrability of the reciprocal of a continuous nonvanishing function
Module: Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Integrals
Name: intervalIntegral.intervalIntegrable_inv
Description: Let $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function on the closed interval $[a, b]$ such that $f(x) \neq 0$ for all $x \in [a, b]$. Then the function $x \mapsto f(x)^{-1}$ is interval integrable on $[a, b]$ with respect to any measure $\mu$ on $\mathbb{R}$.
Signature:  (h : ∀ x : ℝ, x ∈ [[a, b]] → f x ≠ 0) (hf : ContinuousOn f [[a, b]]) : IntervalIntegrable (fun x => (f x)⁻¹) μ a b

4. [THEOREM] Integral of Indicator of Constant Function Equals Scaled Measure
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.SetIntegral
Name: MeasureTheory.integral_indicator_const
Description: For any complete space $E$ and constant $e \in E$, and for any measurable set $s$, the integral of the indicator function of $s$ applied to the constant function $x \mapsto e$ over the whole space equals the scalar product of the measure $\mu(s)$ (converted to a real number) and $e$. That is,
\[ \int_X \mathbb{1}_s(x) e \, d\mu = (\mu s)_{\text{toReal}} \cdot e. \]
Signature:  [CompleteSpace E] (e : E) ⦃s : Set X⦄ (s_meas : MeasurableSet s) :
  ∫ x : X, s.indicator (fun _ : X => e) x ∂μ = (μ s).toReal • e
