2. [THEOREM] Norm of integral bounded by integral of norm over ordered interval
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral
Name: intervalIntegral.norm_integral_le_integral_norm
Description: For any real numbers $a \leq b$ and any function $f \colon \mathbb{R} \to E$, the norm of the integral of $f$ over the interval $[a, b]$ is bounded above by the integral of the norm of $f$ over the same interval. That is,
\[ \left\| \int_{a}^{b} f(x) \, d\mu \right\| \leq \int_{a}^{b} \|f(x)\| \, d\mu. \]
Signature:  (h : a ≤ b) : ‖∫ x in a..b, f x ∂μ‖ ≤ ∫ x in a..b, ‖f x‖ ∂μ

3. [THEOREM] Nonnegativity of Interval Integral Under Almost Everywhere Nonnegative Condition
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral
Name: intervalIntegral.integral_nonneg_of_ae
Description: For any real numbers $a \leq b$ and any measurable function $f$ with respect to a measure $\mu$, if $f$ is almost everywhere nonnegative (i.e., $0 \leq f(x)$ for $\mu$-almost every $x \in \mathbb{R}$), then the interval integral of $f$ from $a$ to $b$ is nonnegative, i.e.,
\[ \int_{a}^{b} f(x) \, d\mu \geq 0. \]
Signature:  (hab : a ≤ b) (hf : 0 ≤ᵐ[μ] f) : 0 ≤ ∫ u in a..b, f u ∂μ

4. [THEOREM] Interval integral over ordered endpoints equals integral over half-open interval
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral
Name: intervalIntegral.integral_of_le
Description: For any real numbers $a$ and $b$ with $a \leq b$, the interval integral $\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \, d\mu$ is equal to the integral of $f$ over the half-open interval $(a, b]$, i.e.,
\[ \int_{a}^{b} f(x) \, d\mu = \int_{(a,b]} f(x) \, d\mu. \]
Signature:  (h : a ≤ b) : ∫ x in a..b, f x ∂μ = ∫ x in Ioc a b, f x ∂μ

5. [THEOREM] Monotonicity of Interval Integral under Pointwise Inequality
Module: Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral
Name: intervalIntegral.integral_mono
Description: For any measurable functions $f, g : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ and real numbers $a, b$, if $f(x) \leq g(x)$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$, then the interval integral of $f$ from $a$ to $b$ is less than or equal to the interval integral of $g$ from $a$ to $b$, i.e.,
\[ \int_{a}^{b} f(u) \, d\mu \leq \int_{a}^{b} g(u) \, d\mu. \]
Signature:  (h : f ≤ g) : (∫ u in a..b, f u ∂μ) ≤ ∫ u in a..b, g u ∂μ

