\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} < 2(\sqrt{2}-1),
\]

so the strict inequality holds. Moreover, equality cannot occur because the integrand is strictly decreasing on \([1,2]\); the integral of a strictly decreasing positive function over an interval of positive length is always strictly larger than its value at the left endpoint. Therefore, from the true non‑strict inequality \( \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \le \int_{1}^{2} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx \) we can indeed conclude the strict inequality \( \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} < \int_{1}^{2} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx \).

Thus the theorem statement is mathematically correct.
</justification>
